    "comment": "HPr (histidine containing protein [Kundig71a]) is one of two sugar-non-specific protein constituents of the phosphoenolpyruvate:sugar phosphotransferase system (PTSsugar).  It accepts a phosphoryl group from phosphorylated Enzyme I (PtsI-P) and then transfers it to the EIIA domain of any one of the many sugar-specific enzymes (collectively known as Enzymes II) of the PTSsugar (reviewed in [Postma93]).\n\nHPr is a small monomeric, single domain, protein that is relatively heat stable [Anderson71a]. HPr is phosphorylated at the N-1 position of histidine residue 15 (His-15) [Anderson71a, Rajagopal94]. Phosphorylated HPr transfers its phosphate to the active histidine of the sugar transporters of the PTSsugar. Solution structures of the complex between HPr and the N-terminal domain of Enzyme I [Garrett99], and between HPr and the EIIA domain/protein of the glucose-specific PTS [Wang00f], the mannitol PTS [Cornilescu02] and the mannose PTS [Williams05a] have been obtained. HPr uses a consensus surface to interact with the varying proteins of the PTSsugar [Wang00j]. \n\nPreferential utilization of glucose over mannitol is dependent upon HPr; dephosphorylated HPr, which accumulates during glucose transport, interacts specifically with MtlR and enhances its repressor activity resulting in the inhibition of mannitol utilization [Choe17] (further [Choe19]).    \n\nPurified HPr binds to, and enhances the oligomeric associations of glycogen phosphorylase (GlgP); HPr stimulates GlgP activity in vitro (decreased Km for glycogen; increased Vmax for phosphate); phosphorylated HPr has 4-fold higher affinity for GlgP than the unphosphorylated form, but does not appear to stimulate GlgP activity [Seok97] (see also [Seok01]). The unphosphorylated form of HPr binds to the anti-sigma factor, Rsd with high affinity; dephosphorylated HPr interacts with and sequesters Rsd from \u03c370 in vitro; dephosphorylated HPr antagonizes Rsd activity resulting in increased \u03c370 dependent transcription and decreased \u03c3S-dependent transcription in vivo [Park13a] (see also [Park15c]). HPr copurifies with the AI-2 kinase LsrK; HPr inhibits LsrK activity in vitro; phosphorylated HPr has a reduced ability to inhibit LsrK activity; HPr mediated regulation of LsrK may link sugar metabolism to quorum sensing activity [Ha18]. HPr is an allosteric activator of pyruvate kinase 1, 6-phosphofructokinase 2 and glucosamine-6-phosphate deaminase; phosphorylated HPr is an allosteric inhibitor of adenylate kinase [Rodionova17, Rodionova18]. \n\nBoth of the general PTS proteins - PtsI and PtsH - localize mainly near the cell poles [Lopian10].   \n\nPhosphoryl flux through PTS systems (via Enzyme I and HPr) plays a regulatory role in coordinating carbon utilization strategy and more specifically in switching between the two alternative metabolic strategies of hierarchical sugar utilization versus co-utilization [Westermayer16].\n\nReviews: [Waygood98]\nRelated reviews: [Peterkofsky01, Gabor11, Escalante12]\n\n"
  },
